LEAD = {
    "headline": "OGILVY CAN NOW PUT CAA'S ACTORS AND ATHLETES IN BRAND ADVERTS WITHOUT THEM TURNING UP, AT 65 TO 75 PERCENT LESS PER ASSET, AND THE THING THAT MADE IT POSSIBLE IS NOT THE AI.  IT IS A SCANNING ROOM WITH 204 CAMERAS THAT CAA BUILT FOUR YEARS AGO",
    "deck": "Ogilvy and CAA announced a product called Echo yesterday.  It makes advertising featuring AI generated versions of CAA's roster, and Ogilvy expects it to produce more assets, faster, at 65 to 75 percent lower cost per asset.  Read the detail and the story changes shape.  The scans have existed for four years.  What arrived this week is the permission to use them.",
    "stamps": [
        ("DIGIDAY  9 NOV", "https://digiday.com/marketing/ogilvy-and-caa-launch-partnership-to-clone-celebrity-talent-for-ai-ads/"),
        ("MARKETING DIVE  9 NOV", "https://www.marketingdive.com/news/how-ogilvy-uses-ai-to-unlock-scale-protect-rights-for-brand-talent-shoots/874014/"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "Here is the deal.  WPP's Ogilvy has signed a partnership with Creative Artists Agency, the talent agency that represents a large share of Hollywood, to make brand advertising starring AI generated replicas of CAA's actors, athletes and creators.  The product is called <mark>Echo</mark>.  Terry Crews has opted in.  Ogilvy's Carol Reed, its global chief innovation officer, says <mark>a few pilots are being negotiated now and should go live towards the end of the year</mark>, and she declined to name the clients.  The partnership took a year to put together.",
        "The number everybody will quote is the cost one.  Echo is expected to yield more assets than a normal shoot, at a faster pace, <mark>reducing cost per asset by 65 to 75 percent</mark>.  Worth being precise about what that is: it is Ogilvy's own expectation for a product that has not run a campaign yet, not a measured result from finished work.  Nobody has published a tested figure, because there is nothing to test.",
        "Now the part that actually matters.  Echo gives Ogilvy access to <mark>The Vault</mark>, a scanning facility the technology company Veritone built for CAA <mark>four years ago</mark>.  It uses <mark>204 cameras</mark> to capture everything about a performer, voice, body language, facial expression, and the way they walk.  CAA clients refresh their scan <mark>every three to five years</mark>.  And that material has already been in use for years, for de-aging actors and fixing shots in films.  Ogilvy can now pull it into WPP Open, the system behind the holding company's production and creative tools, for storyboards, mock-ups or finished assets.",
        "So the generating was not the hard part, and it has not been for a while.  Coca-Cola and Snickers both ran campaigns earlier this year featuring a digital clone of the football manager Jose Mourinho, who is a CAA client.  Ogilvy used digital scans of Shah Rukh Khan to make Diwali adverts for small businesses across India five years ago, and Reed calls that project the prototype for Echo.  What was missing was a way for a brand to commission this without negotiating the likeness, the consent, the approvals and the markets from scratch every single time.",
        "That is what got built.  <mark>CAA clients own their digital likeness</mark>, choose which businesses and which markets they will appear in, and, in Reed's words, always get to see everything and approve everything at the end.  Every AI asset carries a <mark>watermark marking it as both AI generated and approved by the star</mark>, which Reed says is there to tell audiences this is not a deep fake, and to stay ahead of regulation.  The European Union has required watermarks on generated content since August.  CAA signed a deal with YouTube in 2024 to pull celebrity deep fakes off the platform.  The rights machinery is the product.  The model is a feature of it.",
        "The audience has not been consulted and does not sound keen.  <mark>49 percent of Britons take a negative view of AI generated actors appearing in adverts</mark>, and <mark>92 percent say AI generated content makes it harder to trust information online</mark>, both from YouGov figures Digiday cites.  Gartner's Nicole Greene puts the problem plainly, that the whole point of talent is to scale authentic relationships and credibility, while also saying agencies need a first-mover advantage right now.  Reed's answer is that Ogilvy still expects to recommend a real shoot when the situation demands one.  The most interesting thing she said is about who gets hired: she hopes brands will open up to <mark>B-tier athletes</mark>, because the scheduling problem that made them hard to use just went away.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("65-75%", "the reduction in cost per asset Ogilvy expects from Echo, which is a forecast for a product with no completed campaigns, not a measured result"),
        ("204", "cameras in The Vault, the CAA scanning facility Veritone built four years ago, capturing voice, body language, facial expression and gait"),
        ("49% / 92%", "of Britons view AI generated actors in adverts negatively, and say AI generated content makes online information harder to trust, per YouGov figures cited by Digiday"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "The cost figure is Ogilvy's own forward-looking expectation, reported by Digiday, not an independently measured result, and no Echo campaign has launched.  Reed declined to name any client.  Terry Crews is the only performer publicly confirmed as opted in.  The YouGov figures are quoted by Digiday without field dates, sample size or margin of error in the piece, so treat them as directional.  The argument here that the rights framework rather than the AI is the thing that changed is reasoning on the reported facts, not a claim Ogilvy or CAA have made.  Nobody has published what talent are paid for a scan licence, so the cost saving per asset tells you nothing about the total cost of a campaign.",
    "so_what": "Strip out the novelty and this is a rights deal wearing an AI jacket.  The scanning room is four years old and the clone adverts already ran this year, so the technology was never the constraint.  The constraint was that commissioning one meant a bespoke negotiation over likeness, consent, approval and territory, every time, which is slow and expensive and scares legal teams.  CAA and Ogilvy have turned that into something closer to a standing licence with a watermark on the end.  Which means the question to put to a talent agency has changed.  It is no longer whether they can generate it.  It is whether a licensed scan exists, who signs off, and in which markets.  And the honest tension sits in the Gartner line: you buy a famous face to borrow a real relationship, and half the public already says a generated one puts them off.",
    "do_this": "Ask any talent agency you are about to brief three questions before you talk money: does this person have a licensed digital scan, who approves each asset, and which markets and categories are already cleared.  The answers tell you your real production timeline.  Then write your own disclosure line now, before a client asks for one, and decide whether a watermark is the whole of it or whether you say it on screen.  And if you have been pricing a single big name because scheduling ruled out the people you actually wanted, go back and price three smaller ones instead, because the scheduling argument has just lost its force.",
}

